  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T13:00-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/515f-400o400o1-p61Tqcqyzihra23gvP7GAS.png",
   :session/title
   "Smuggling Clojure into Statically Compiled Video Games with Silk",
   :session/description
   "If you're anything like me, you got into programming to make video games. Eventually you found the Lispest Lisp, and now you can't imagine building a game without your REPL. But everyone knows a \"real\" game ships to Steam, PlayStation, and Nintendo, and for various wise reasons, existing Clojure implementations do not.\r\n\r\nBeing immune to wisdom, I lobotomized ClojureCLR and Lua and glued them together with a new runtime and a JIT-enabled bytecode compiler, keeping ClojureCLR's data structures. Silk is a Clojure able to ship to Desktop, iOS, Android, Xbox, Nintendo, and PlayStation. It keeps eval, defmacro, and a live REPL on locked-down platforms without the runtime code generation every other Clojure needs to boot. As a fun side effect, it can compile static binaries, like Go, via a self-hosted compiler.\r\n\r\nVisit with some language hackery and join me at the end of this 10-year rabbit hole to uncover what it takes to smuggle Clojure into statically compiled video games.\n\n## James J. Tolton\n\nIndie game dev, content creator, and language hacker. Author of libapl-clj, libscryer-clj, and scrypy, designer of libpython-clj's user-facing API, and architect of the Silk programming language.",
   :session/external-id "1263247",
   :session/subtitle "James J. Tolton",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T13:00-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/0734-400o400o1-QWB6KY5BgHn4x4bHLy5joB.jpg",
   :session/title "A GIS DSL Across the JVM and the Browser",
   :session/description
   "A major U.S. city's zoning update needed maps and analyses beyond what worked well for conventional geospatial tools. The domain-specific language in this talk grew from that need: citywide regulatory analyses over many spatial operations spread across thousands of geospatial parcels, running on the JVM and in the browser from one codebase. Underneath sit small Clojure libraries that each port a familiar JVM idiom to the JS ecosystem: native interop described as data, deterministic resource disposal, and core.async.flow-style parallelism. The talk works the stack through the reimplementations, showing which Clojure feature does the work at each layer and where the abstractions have salient differences.\n\n## Will Cohen\n\nWill Cohen is an urban planner on the Zoning Reform team at the City of Boston's Planning Department, where he works on the city's zoning code, including legalizing accessory dwelling units. He has used Clojure since 2016.",
   :session/external-id "1263921",
   :session/subtitle "Will Cohen",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T13:50-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/7719-400o400o1-PyHBMw6Q2Rt1f5ivwZHbdM.jpg",
   :session/title "Modeling a Fabric Stash with Datomic",
   :session/description
   "A fabric collection breaks the tidy assumptions data-modeling tutorials make. One bolt is a single solid color. Another is a cotton-linen blend in several colors. Some carry a fabric type, while many don't. This talk uses that genuinely messy domain to show why Datomic's attribute-based schema fits sparse, irregular data better than rigid relational tables. We'll walk through a real fabric stash schema covering fibers, structures, colors, and weights, modeled as enumerated values, with single- and multi-valued attributes side by side. From there, we watch it grow as new fabric types appear, without a migration. You'll leave with concrete techniques for modeling any irregular, real-world domain in Datomic, instead of forcing it into columns that don't fit.\n\n## Wendy Randolph\n\nWendy is a freelance software developer, community speaker, and conference host, passionate about functional programming and helping others break into tech. A career-changer and founding student of Clojure Camp, she has hosted Babashka Conf, Clojure Conj, and ElixirConf, and spoken at functional programming events around the world. Currently, she's building with Elixir professionally and staying active in Clojure. When not behind a computer or a microphone, she enjoys sewing, jazz dance, sports cars, and Disney.",
   :session/external-id "1263994",
   :session/subtitle "Wendy Randolph",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T13:50-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/4eae-400o400o1-hYocqAceZHMGSQ1qsj3Gus.jpg",
   :session/title "Teaching Automata Theory with Clojure",
   :session/description
   "Teaching the theory of computation often suffers from a disconnect between mathematical models and practical software engineering. This experience report details a classroom-tested approach refined over five years, showing how Clojure bridges that divide by enabling undergraduate CS students to build live, functional simulators for abstract machines, from basic regular expressions to finite automata and Turing machines. By shifting from dry, textbook theory to an experimental, data-driven methodology, this framework has dramatically enhanced engagement and deepened students’ architectural understanding of language semantics. Attendees will learn how Clojure’s core functional strengths turn intimidating theoretical concepts into tangible, executable code, leaving with a proven pedagogical blueprint that inspires and empowers the next generation of computer scientists and software developers.\n\n## Ariel Ortiz\n\nAriel Ortiz is a programming language enthusiast. Since 1994, he has been a full-time faculty member at Tecnológico de Monterrey in Mexico, where he has primarily been involved in teaching undergraduate computer science courses. His areas of interest include programming languages, compiler construction, and web development. He is an active member of the ACM’s special interest group on computer science education, SIGCSE. In 2010, he began teaching Clojure in his classes, but his experience teaching with Lisp-like languages started with Scheme over thirty years ago.",
   :session/external-id "1263959",
   :session/subtitle "Ariel Ortiz",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
